# Ragtime Blues in C (repertoire plan: st-ragtime-blues-c). An original fingerpicked study on the
# ragtime cycle of dominant sevenths, C | C | A7 | A7 | D7 | D7 | G7 | G7, played twice (bars 1-16),
# with a one-bar ending on C (bar 17) so the piece comes home instead of stopping on G7.
# The thumb (P) plays every beat and alternates two bass strings in the same order on every chord:
# root, the next string up, the alternate bass, the next string up again.
#   C (x32010):  string 5 fret 3, string 4 fret 2, string 6 fret 3, string 4 fret 2 (finger 3 hops from string 5 to 6)
#   A7 (x02020): string 5 open, string 4 fret 2, string 6 open, string 4 fret 2
#   D7 (xx0212): string 4 open, string 3 fret 2, string 5 open, string 3 fret 2
#   G7 (320001): string 6 fret 3, string 4 open, string 5 fret 2, string 4 open
# The tune is on strings 1-3 (strings 1-2 in the D7 bars, where the thumb uses string 3). It is
# syncopated: many tune notes fall on the "and" and are tied over the next beat while the thumb
# carries on underneath. The guide tones move by half steps across the chords: C# (A7) to C (D7)
# to B (G7) to C, and bars 8 and 16 climb D, D# to the E that starts the next bar.
# Four tune notes arrive early, on the last "and" of a bar, and are tied into the next chord:
# F# into D7 (bar 4), B into G7 (bars 6 and 14) and C# into A7 (bar 10, where C rises to C#).
# Extra notes above the shapes: finger 4 takes string 1 fret 3 (G) and string 2 fret 3 (D), and
# string 2 fret 4 (D#) in bars 8 and 16; finger 3 takes string 1 fret 2 (F#) over A7 and D7, and
# string 2 fret 2 (C#) at the end of bar 10.
title: Ragtime Blues in C
composer: Sixstring Academy
source: Original fingerstyle study on the ragtime progression
licence: Original study for Sixstring Academy
difficulty: 7
tags: ragtime, fingerstyle, alternating bass, syncopation, dominant sevenths
tempo: 100
time: 4/4
key: C

track: Your part
role: lead
